---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
---

<Layout 
  title="Optics Cheat Sheet | Free Geometric, Wave, Quantum Optics"
  description="Complete optics cheat sheet with geometric, wave, and quantum optics. Free download for physics students."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Optics<br><span>Cheat Sheet</span></h1>
    <p>Geometric, wave, and quantum optics. From ray tracing to photonics.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Geometric Optics</h2>
    <div class="formula-grid">
      <div class="formula-card">
        <h3>Reflection & Refraction</h3>
        <pre><code>θ_i = θ_r (reflection)
n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ (Snell's law)
n = c/v (refractive index)
θ_c = sin⁻¹(n₂/n₁) (critical angle)</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-card">
        <h3>Lenses</h3>
        <pre><code>1/f = 1/v + 1/u (lens formula)
M = -v/u (magnification)
Power = 1/f (dioptres)
Thin lens&#58; 1/f = (n-1)(1/R₁ - 1/R₂)</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Wave Optics</h2>
    <div class="formula-grid">
      <div class="formula-card">
        <h3>Interference</h3>
        <pre><code>Constructive&#58; Δ = mλ
Destructive&#58; Δ = (m+½)λ
Young's slits&#58; x = λL/d
Thin films&#58; 2nt = mλ (with phase change)</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-card">
        <h3>Diffraction</h3>
        <pre><code>Single slit&#58; a sin θ = mλ (minima)
Grating&#58; d sin θ = mλ (maxima)
Resolving power&#58; R = λ/Δλ = mN
Rayleigh criterion&#58; θ = 1.22λ/D</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Polarisation</h2>
    <div class="formula-grid">
      <div class="formula-card">
        <h3>Malus's Law</h3>
        <pre><code>I = I₀ cos²θ
θ&#58; angle between polariser and analyser
Unpolarised → polarised&#58; I = I₀/2</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Quantum Optics</h2>
    <div class="formula-grid">
      <div class="formula-card">
        <h3>Photons</h3>
        <pre><code>E = hf = hc/λ
p = h/λ = E/c
Photoelectric&#58; hf = φ + KE_max
Compton&#58; Δλ = (h/m_e c)(1 - cos θ)</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Download</h2>
    <p>Print this page or save as PDF for quick reference.</p>
    <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Use Ctrl+P (Cmd+P on Mac) to print or save as PDF.</p>
  </section>
</Layout>
